(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-abdominal-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Abdominal Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The 'new order' of the abdominal exam sequence taught in lecture is...","choices":["Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation, Inspection","Auscultation, Inspection, Palpation, Percussion","Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation","Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion, Palpation"],"answer":3,"exp":"The taught sequence is inspection first, then auscultation, then percussion, then palpation — auscultation is deliberately performed before percussion/palpation so that bowel sounds aren't artificially altered by prior manipulation of the abdomen. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Auscultation is performed before percussion and palpation in the abdominal exam specifically because...","choices":["auscultation is simply faster to perform and therefore saves overall clinical exam time","patients generally tolerate auscultation better when it is performed last, after palpation","percussion and palpation are technically not standard parts of the abdominal exam sequence","percussing or palpating first can alter bowel sound activity, giving a misleading auscultation result"],"answer":3,"exp":"Mechanically stimulating the bowel via percussion or palpation can transiently increase peristalsis/bowel sounds, so auscultation is performed first to get an accurate baseline assessment of bowel activity. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"Purple striae noted on abdominal inspection can be associated with...","choices":["a completely normal finding in all patients","only recent weight loss with no other significance","normal aging with no clinical correlation","liver disease and dilated veins (caput medusae)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Purple striae are associated with conditions like liver disease and can accompany the dilated periumbilical veins known as caput medusae, both of which may be visible on abdominal inspection. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"On abdominal inspection, a pulsation visible in the epigastrium is most likely to represent...","choices":["normal aortic pulsation, which can be visible in thin patients","a definite sign of active bowel obstruction requiring urgent workup","always an abnormal finding requiring immediate surgical intervention","never a normal finding, regardless of the patient's body habitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aortic pulsation may be normally visible in the epigastrium, particularly in thin patients, and is a recognized normal inspection finding distinct from abnormal peristaltic waves seen in obstruction. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Normal bowel sounds are described as clicks and gurgles occurring at a rate of approximately...","choices":["1 to 2 per hour","5 to 34 per minute","exactly 10 per minute, with no variation","100 to 200 per minute"],"answer":1,"exp":"Normal bowel sounds (clicks and gurgles) occur at a rate of roughly 5 to 34 per minute, a fairly wide normal range. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Borborygmi refers to...","choices":["a coarse grating friction rub heard with respiration over the liver surface","a whooshing vascular bruit heard with the diaphragm over the aorta","prolonged gurgles of hyperperistalsis, informally known as 'stomach growling'","the complete and prolonged absence of any audible bowel sounds at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Borborygmi are prolonged, loud gurgling sounds reflecting hyperperistalsis — what's commonly called 'stomach growling.' — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"If bowel sounds appear absent on initial listening, the examiner should...","choices":["immediately document 'absent' after only a few brief seconds of silence","listen for one full minute in each quadrant before documenting them as truly absent","switch immediately to percussion instead, abandoning further auscultation","skip auscultation of the remaining three quadrants entirely and move on"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bowel sounds can be intermittent, so the examiner must listen for a full minute in each of the four quadrants before concluding and documenting that bowel sounds are truly absent. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"When documenting bowel sound findings, the four possible descriptive categories are...","choices":["absent, normoactive, hyperactive, hypoactive","loud, soft, medium, silent","mild, moderate, severe, critical","only present or absent, with no other categories"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bowel sound findings are documented using one of four standard categories: absent, normoactive, hyperactive, or hypoactive. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"When listening for vascular bruits over the aorta, renal, iliac, and femoral arteries, the examiner should use...","choices":["the bell of the stethoscope","the diaphragm of the stethoscope","the stethoscope tubing pressed directly on skin","no stethoscope at all, only palpation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bruits are best heard using the diaphragm of the stethoscope over the aorta, renal arteries, iliac arteries, and femoral arteries. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"A friction rub heard over the liver or spleen is a rare finding that indicates...","choices":["significant systemic dehydration and volume depletion","a completely normal vascular sound heard at rest in everyone","entirely normal, healthy bowel peristaltic activity","inflammation of the peritoneal surface of that organ"],"answer":3,"exp":"A friction rub is a grating sound heard with respiration over the liver or spleen, indicating inflammation of that organ's peritoneal surface — a rare but clinically significant finding. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"A venous hum, heard over the epigastrium or umbilicus as a soft humming sound in both systole and diastole, indicates...","choices":["acute appendicitis with localized peritoneal inflammation and marked rebound tenderness","increased collateral circulation between the portal and systemic venous systems, as in hepatic cirrhosis","entirely normal peristaltic bowel activity heard consistently throughout the healthy abdomen","a completely normal vascular finding expected to be present in all healthy, unaffected adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"A venous hum reflects increased collateral blood flow between the portal and systemic venous systems, classically associated with hepatic cirrhosis and portal hypertension. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"During abdominal percussion, tympany typically predominates because of the presence of...","choices":["gas within hollow organs","solid organs exclusively","dense muscle tissue","fluid collections exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Tympany (a hollow, drum-like percussion note) predominates over most of the abdomen because gas within the hollow GI (gastrointestinal) organs is the most common underlying content, in contrast to dullness, which occurs over fluid, feces, or solid organs. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"If an area of unexpected dullness is discovered during abdominal percussion, the examiner should...","choices":["ignore it entirely, since dullness is always considered a normal exam finding","percuss around the area to define its extent, considering an underlying mass or enlarged organ","immediately stop the entire abdominal exam and refer the patient onward for imaging","switch immediately to auscultation only, abandoning further percussion technique"],"answer":1,"exp":"Unexpected dullness should prompt the examiner to percuss around the area to map its borders/extent, helping determine whether it reflects an underlying mass or organomegaly. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"To measure the vertical span of liver dullness in the right midclavicular line, the examiner starts percussing below the umbilicus and moves upward to find...","choices":["the upper border of liver dullness","the splenic percussion sign","CVA (costovertebral angle) tenderness","the lower border of liver dullness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Starting below the umbilicus and percussing upward (from tympany toward dullness) identifies the lower border of liver dullness, which is then combined with the separately-determined upper border to calculate liver span. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"To identify the upper border of liver dullness in the midclavicular line, the examiner starts at the nipple line and percusses...","choices":["only over the left side of the chest and abdomen","downward, from lung resonance toward liver dullness","side to side across the abdomen at each rib level","upward, from the umbilicus toward the nipple line"],"answer":1,"exp":"The upper border of liver dullness is found by starting at the nipple line (over resonant lung tissue) and percussing downward until the note changes from resonance to dullness, marking the upper liver border. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"Percussion for the spleen along the left costal margin (Traube's space) should normally reveal a change from tympany to dullness occurring...","choices":["only at the midline of the abdomen","only at the midclavicular line","it should remain tympanic everywhere, with no area of dullness","between the anterior axillary line and mid-axillary line"],"answer":3,"exp":"In a normal-sized spleen, the percussion note changes from tympany to dullness within the expected zone between the anterior axillary line and mid-axillary line; dullness extending beyond this zone suggests splenomegaly. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"The splenic percussion sign is performed by percussing the left anterior axillary line in the lowest intercostal space and asking the patient to take a deep breath. A normal (negative) result shows...","choices":["complete absence of any percussion note","no change in the percussion note during inhalation","a loud bruit heard instead of a percussion note","a change from tympany to dullness during inhalation"],"answer":1,"exp":"In a normal (negative) splenic percussion sign, the percussion note in this area does not change from tympany to dullness during inhalation; a change to dullness with inspiration (positive sign) suggests splenic enlargement. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Light palpation of the abdomen is performed to a depth of approximately...","choices":["5 to 10 cm","less than 1 cm","there is no defined depth for light palpation","3 to 4 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Light palpation is a superficial technique, pressing to a depth of less than 1 cm, used to identify tenderness, muscular resistance, and superficial organs/masses before deeper palpation. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"During light palpation, the examiner's technique should involve...","choices":["pressing firmly downward, roughly 3-4 cm deep into the abdomen, using the whole flat hand in one motion","using only the isolated fingertips individually, never engaging the entire flat hand at once together","a vertical hand plane with the fingers spread widely apart, pressing straight downward with firm pressure","a horizontal hand plane, fingers together, using a light gentle dipping motion, barely raising the hand off the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Light palpation technique specifically calls for the hand held in a horizontal plane with fingers together, using a light, gentle dipping motion, barely lifting the hand off the skin between presses. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"During both light and deep palpation, any area of known tenderness should be examined...","choices":["first, before any of the other abdominal areas","last, after all other areas have been assessed","using the same technique, regardless of the chosen order","only if the patient specifically requests it directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"To avoid causing pain that could make the patient guard and complicate the rest of the exam, the area of known/suspected tenderness should always be palpated last. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"Deep palpation of the abdomen, performed with two hands using the palmar surfaces of the fingers, presses to a depth of approximately...","choices":["10 cm or more","3 to 4 cm","deep palpation uses no measurable depth target","less than 1 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Deep palpation presses to a depth of about 3 to 4 cm using the palmar surfaces of the fingers, typically bimanually, in all four quadrants, to better assess for masses or organomegaly beyond what light palpation can detect. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"When palpating the liver using the standard bimanual technique, the examiner's left hand is used to...","choices":["percuss the liver span","press directly on the right upper quadrant to feel for the liver edge","auscultate for bowel sounds","lift gently under the patient's right flank"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the standard bimanual liver palpation technique, the left hand is placed under the patient's right flank and gently lifts upward, while the right hand palpates the abdomen searching for the liver edge as the patient breathes deeply. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"During bimanual liver palpation, the patient is asked to take a deep breath while the examiner...","choices":["asks the patient to hold their breath firmly in place before any palpation of the abdomen even begins","releases all applied pressure entirely, removing the hand from skin contact completely and fully","presses gently inward and upward with the right hand, feeling for the liver edge as it descends with inspiration","moves the right hand entirely away from the right side, over to the left upper quadrant instead"],"answer":2,"exp":"As the diaphragm descends with a deep breath, the liver moves downward; the examiner presses gently inward and upward with the right hand at that moment to feel the liver edge slide against the fingertips. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"If the liver edge is not felt on the first attempt at its expected location, the examiner should...","choices":["stop the entire abdominal exam immediately and completely, without attempting any further palpation maneuvers","immediately and confidently conclude that the liver is congenitally absent from the abdomen entirely","move the right hand upward and repeat, continuing until the liver edge is felt or the right costal margin is reached","move the palpating hand over to the left side of the abdomen instead, searching there for the edge"],"answer":2,"exp":"If the liver edge isn't felt initially, the examiner systematically moves the palpating hand upward and repeats the maneuver, continuing until either the liver edge is felt or the right costal margin is reached. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"The 'hooking technique' for liver palpation involves the examiner...","choices":["standing to the patient's left side, facing directly toward the patient's head the entire time","pressing straight downward firmly on the abdomen without ever hooking the fingers underneath the ribs","using only a single hand throughout the entire maneuver, with the other hand never involved at all","standing to the right, facing the patient's feet, and hooking both hands' fingers under the right costal margin"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the hooking technique, the examiner stands to the patient's right, facing toward the patient's feet, and hooks the fingers of both hands under the right costal margin, asking the patient to take a deep breath while palpating for the liver edge. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"A key teaching point about the normal spleen on physical exam is that it is...","choices":["percussed using the midclavicular line, like the liver","located in the right upper quadrant","always easily palpable in every patient","usually not palpable"],"answer":3,"exp":"In a normal, healthy adult, the spleen is usually not palpable; feeling a spleen tip generally raises suspicion for splenomegaly. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"During spleen palpation, the examiner tries to feel the tip of the spleen touch the fingertips specifically...","choices":["during exhalation only, as the spleen ascends with the diaphragm","it cannot ever be felt on physical exam under any circumstances","during inhalation, as the spleen descends with the diaphragm","only when the patient is standing fully upright throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Similar to liver palpation, the spleen (when enlarged enough to be palpable) is felt touching the examiner's fingertips during inhalation, as it descends along with diaphragmatic movement. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Regarding kidney palpability on exam, a key teaching point is that...","choices":["kidneys are generally non-palpable, though the right kidney (being lower than the left) may be felt in a thin patient","both kidneys are easily and reliably palpable in essentially all adult patients, regardless of build","the left kidney, which is said to sit lower than the right, is more often palpable in a thin, cooperative patient","kidney palpation requires no particular technique at all and can be performed casually by any examiner"],"answer":0,"exp":"Kidneys are generally not palpable on exam; however, because the right kidney sits somewhat lower than the left (due to the liver above it), it may occasionally be felt in a thin patient using proper technique. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 47"},{"q":"The bimanual kidney 'capture' technique involves pushing the left hand upward under the flank while, at the peak of inspiration, the right hand...","choices":["presses firmly and deeply downward to trap the kidney between both hands","percusses firmly over the kidney region instead of applying pressure","auscultates carefully for renal artery bruits at that moment","is removed from contact with the abdomen entirely at that point"],"answer":0,"exp":"At the peak of inspiration (when the kidney has descended as far as it will), the right hand presses firmly and deeply downward, attempting to 'capture' the kidney between the two hands before it is released as the patient exhales. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 48"},{"q":"CVA (costovertebral angle) tenderness is assessed by...","choices":["using only light superficial palpation over the flank region, without ever incorporating any percussive component","placing a palm against the costovertebral angle and delivering a sharp blow to that hand with a fist, then asking about tenderness","applying deep bimanual palpation with both hands simultaneously pressed firmly against both flanks at the same time","auscultating with the stethoscope diaphragm placed directly over the costovertebral angle region on each side"],"answer":1,"exp":"With the patient sitting and the examiner standing behind, the examiner places a palm flat against the costovertebral angle and strikes that hand with the other fist (a fist percussion technique), then asks the patient about any resulting tenderness — repeated on both sides. — Physical Examination of the Abdomen lecture, Slide 51"}]};
